@Andrzej

Draw a line down the center of Europe, and Albania is in the eastern half.  I think that at least for my generation, Eastern Europe was any country that was under Communist rule during the Cold War.  We hardly ever think of Europe as Northern and Southern. If we consider anything "Northern" at all, it's just Scandinavia and Finland. And "Southern" is particularly problematic, since most of the constituent countries are not contiguous (Spain/Italy/Greece) and because countries like France and Switzerland are ambiguous:  France is about split down the middle, and Switzerland is geographically in the southern half of Europe but doesn't seem "Southern" at all.

So let's split the difference and say Southeastern.

@Steve L

I'm not disputing Albania is closer to the Eastern reaches of Europe than to the Western ones. I'm just saying I never once in my life thought of Albania as Eastern European, culturally speaking.

The distinction between Southern and Northern Europe is one I think most Europeans grasp naturally, and it roughly corresponds to the division between Mediterranean (Romance, roughly, but also Greek and, yes, Turkish) and "other European" culture (Germanic, Slavic, etc.). The cuisine, the outdoor living, the introvertism/extrovertism of the people, the daily rhythm of life - it all varies between Northern and Southern Europe, probably more than between East and West.

